# Architecture rules

- Identify the current module boundaries and dependency direction before proposing a design.
- Keep domain/business logic independent from UI, transport, framework, and persistence details.
- Prefer explicit contracts and dependency injection at system boundaries.
- Avoid circular dependencies and hidden global state.
- Make side effects visible and isolate them behind adapters or services.
- For cross-service or cross-package changes, document compatibility, rollout, and rollback strategy.
- Introduce a new abstraction only when at least two concrete use cases justify it or the boundary is required for testing/security.
- Record consequential architectural decisions in the repository's existing ADR or design-document format.
- For concurrency, distributed workflows, caching, retries, or queues, define idempotency and failure semantics.
- For any new external dependency, justify necessity, maintenance status, security implications, bundle/runtime cost, and removal path.
